---
import StarlightPage from "@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro";
import { Code } from "@astrojs/starlight/components";
import Landing from "../components/landing.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/wallace-js/wallace";
const tagline = "Faster loading, rendering and development.";
const stackblitzOptions =
  "?ctl=1&devtoolsheight=1&terminalHeight=0&hideExplorer=1&&embed=1&file=src%2Findex.jsx";
const stackblitzLink =
  "https://stackblitz.com/edit/wallace-example" + stackblitzOptions;
const hero = {
  title: "Wallace",
  tagline: `
  <div class="tagline">
    <span>The front-end framework for:</span>
    <ul>
      <li>Faster loading</li>
      <li>Faster rendering</li>
      <li>Faster development</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  `,
  image: {
    html: '<img id="logo" src="/img/wallace-tags.png" alt="Wallace logo" />',
  },
  imageAlt: "Wallace logo",
  actions: [
    {
      text: "Tour",
      link: "docs/tour",
      icon: "rocket",
      attrs: {
        id: "tour-btn",
        class: "primary action",
      },
    },
    {
      text: "Docs",
      link: "docs/",
      icon: "open-book",
      attrs: {
        class: "secondary action",
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: "Wallace",
    template: "splash",
    hero: hero,
  }}
>
  <Landing>
    <section>
      <h2>Faster loading</h2>
      <p>
        Wallace loads faster than <a href="/docs/claims"
          >almost any other framework</a
        > thanks to its microscopic bundles, making it ideal for landing pages.
      </p>
      <p class="caption">
        Bundle size of various implementations of the
        <a href="https://github.com/krausest/js-framework-benchmark">
          js-framework-benchmark</a
        > app:
      </p>
      <canvas id="size-compressed"></canvas>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Faster rendering</h2>
      <p>
        Wallace renders faster than <a href="/docs/claims"
          >most other frameworks</a
        > and lets you fine tune updates, making it ideal for performance-critical
        apps.
      </p>
      <p class="caption">
        Time to create 1000 rows on the
        <a href="https://github.com/krausest/js-framework-benchmark">
          js-framework-benchmark</a
        > app:
      </p>
      <canvas id="run1k"></canvas>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Faster development</h2>

      <p>Wallace feels like a sophisticated modern reactive framework:</p>
      <Code
        lang="tsx"
        code={`
import { mount } from 'wallace'; 

const Counter = ({ count }) => (
  <button onClick={count++}>{count}</button>
);

const CounterList = (counters) => (
  <div watch>
    Total: {counters.reduce((a, c) => a + c.count, 0)}
    <button onClick={counters.push({ count: 1 })}> 
      Add Counter
    </button>
    <Counter.repeat models={counters} />
  </div>
);

mount('main', CounterList, []);`}
      />
      <p>
        But such frameworks invariably add layers of indirection and constraints
        which:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Obscure</strong> their internal operations, which impedes debugging.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Restrict</strong> your control of the DOM, which can limit options
          or affect performance.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Force</strong> awkard patterns, which complicates development.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <p>
        Wallace lets you progressively peel back these layers to reveal a very
        simple architecture which you can fully interact with, resulting in:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>Transparent</strong> operations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Freedom</strong> to override any behaviour.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Natural</strong> development patterns.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        These (plus other goodies) help you develop faster, and have more fun
        doing so!
      </p>
